WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:02.040
Narrator: Ever wonder how food commercials

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:05.730
look so mouthwatering and perfect?

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:09.670
These food shoots can cost
hundreds of thousands of dollars,

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:12.353
all so you'll buy more of these.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.040
Visual engineer, Steve Giralt,

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:18.070
shoots photos and videos

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:20.500
for food and product-based commercials

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.220
in New York City and around the world.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.960
I bring technology

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.260
and kind of traditional
image-making processes together

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.750
in what I call visual engineering.

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:32.940
Narrator: One piece of
that technology: robots.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.833
Or, as they call them, motion controls.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:41.450
Steve's long list of
clients include big brands,

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:45.053
like Hershey's, Budweiser,
Pepsi, and Starbucks.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.510
Of course making food look
so good comes with a price.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.490
A single-day TV shoot can cost

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:54.320
between $50,000 to $100,000.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:00:59.760
Steve: In these food
shoots that we create,

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.820
we have a lot of moving parts.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:06.230
Basically, you'll have, let's
say, one item that's moving,

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:08.980
something else that's
flashing and running into it,

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:10.690
something else that it's gonna land on.

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:13.770
We have to use a lot of
different technologies

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.910
and synchronizing software

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:17.960
to basically allow those
things all to happen

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:20.110
at the exact moment we
need them to happen.

00:01:20.997 --> 00:01:23.488
We love it, here we go.

00:01:23.488 --> 00:01:25.310
Comes out pretty low.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:27.660
Narrator: It also takes
a symphony of people

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:30.063
to make the products look
the best they can be.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:35.570
Various stylists, engineers, and operators

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:38.363
work together to create the perfect shot.

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:42.793
My name is Michelle Gatton,
and I am a food stylist.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:47.640
When I get recipes, I read over the recipe

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:48.900
so I know what I'm gonna be doing.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.420
So, in the back of my head,

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.559
I start thinking about
how I'd like to do it.

00:01:52.559 --> 00:01:56.610
It could change once we get on
set with the different props,

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:58.470
what the art director wants could change,

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.850
but I start trying to create
the recipe in my head,

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:04.030
'cause I'm only makin' it pretty
much the first time on set.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:05.500
I made these brownies yesterday,

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.630
just so we're not waiting
on me making brownies.

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:11.830
And, to make them look
like I just made them,

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:14.390
I just warmed up the
chocolate with a heat gun,

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:16.560
and I'm adding a little
bit of melted chocolate

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:17.900
to the cut side.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.993
Steve: So here we've got
our motorized slider,

00:02:20.993 --> 00:02:23.520
which is moving a pump that's
gonna have caramel on it

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.640
that'll be pumping down onto our brownies.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.620
So, in the end, we'll have this shot

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:29.691
of this beautiful ribbon of caramel

00:02:29.691 --> 00:02:32.690
kind of draping and curling
on top of the brownie.

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:36.014
Narrator: There's a
lot of trial and error.

00:02:36.014 --> 00:02:37.120
Steve: Are you ready?

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:38.786
And here we go.

00:02:38.786 --> 00:02:39.950
Yeah!

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:41.040
Nailed it!

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.970
Narrator: Every precise moment captured

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:45.310
is nailed down to the millisecond.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:48.050
This is the benefit to using robots.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:50.480
Human hands are also used on commercials

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.883
when timing is not a crucial factor.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.770
Steve: We put a lot of
effort into these visuals.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:59.120
It's really cool to see your vision

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.480
kinda come to life and
be spread with the world.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.900
I think you have to have a
really great understanding

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.620
of what is appetizing.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:07.520
Everybody speaks the language of food.

